{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION to my original filing. I first attributed this to a Verified Gate concurrency group that was not keyed per-change, because the first cancellation (04:09:41) coincided to the second with the creation of run 31562353593 for a different change. That was a coincidence, and the hypothesis is refuted by the second occurrence: the mutation job on run 31562494026 ran 04:16:23 -> 04:46:40 and cancelled at 30m17s, matching the first run's 30m16s, with no competing run created at that instant. Two cancellations at the same 30-minute mark is a timeout signature, not a race, and _mutation.yml:34 declares timeout-minutes: 30.\n\nThe proven mechanism is instead the all-shards selection. Reproduced locally against change 1615's diff:\n\n    total shards: 7 -> signing, reducer-processors, next-batch, gates, validate, compact-policy, push-policy\n    changed: .github/workflows/{_eval-discipline,_optionality,external-integration,structured-output-baseline,test}.yml, tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py\n    SELECTED: all 7  => all shards selected? True\n\nbecause .github/workflows/test.yml is listed in global_support and select_shards returns tuple(manifest.shards) whenever ANY changed path matches a global_support pattern.\n\nThe title, description, and acceptance criteria have been rewritten accordingly. Leaving the wrong hypothesis recorded here rather than silently deleting it, since the coincidence is a trap the next person may also fall into: distinguish the two by checking whether the durations cluster at the job's timeout-minutes."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786510294884678001,"uuid":"4d39b403-9cd8-4b33-87f7-9e7a7cf6a530"}